<td id="S1.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S1.T1.5.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">UAT (Universal)</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Without residual connections</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.T1.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu &amp; Sato, 2025</a><span id="S1.T1.5.4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Continuous</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\surd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\surd</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Variant with Hardmax</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.T1.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takakura &amp; Suzuki, 2023</a><span id="S1.T1.5.5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.T1.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">-smooth (Besov)</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\surd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\surd</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Coordinate-wise rate</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.T1.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025b</a><span id="S1.T1.5.6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">​Lipschitz continuous</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\surd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\surd</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Without residual connections</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.T1.5.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2026</a><span id="S1.T1.5.7.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">​Hölder class</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\surd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\surd</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.7.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Without residual connections</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ours</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Hölder class</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\surd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\surd}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\surd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold">√</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\surd}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S1.T1.5.8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Standard Transformers</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As originally proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, a standard Transformer block consists of a self-attention layer with Softmax operator, a feed-forward layer with ReLU activation function, and residual connections in each layer. However, there are almost only UAT results for standard Transformers incorporating these three elements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>. The approximation bounds (or rates) characterizing quantitative relationships between error and complexities of standard Transformers are lacking. Many studies modify Transformers by replacing the Softmax operator with other operators, such as Hardmax operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu &amp; Sato, 2025</a>)</cite> and Maximum operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurevych et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Although both <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2020)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a></cite> transformed the Hardmax into the Softmax by introducing a large multiplicative factor in the Softmax operator, this approach actually leads to extremely large norms of Transformer weight matrices and causes the Lipschitz constants of these transformed layers to explode. Consequently, it will be challenging to maintain a proper trade-off between approximation and statistical errors. Moreover, the Maximum operator differs fundamentally from Softmax in form and function, limiting the result to approximation tasks with Transformer variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurevych et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Further studies established approximation bounds for Softmax Transformers across various function spaces. Notably, theoretical guarantees have been developed for <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth (Besov-like) functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takakura &amp; Suzuki, 2023</a>)</cite>, Lipschitz continuous functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and the broader Hölder class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. However, a prevailing limitation across all these analyses is their reliance on Transformer variants without residual connections in the feed-forward layers. Therefore, approximation upper bounds for general function spaces (e.g., Hölder class) with the standard architectures are lacking. Furthermore, the approximation theory of Transformers remains incomplete — there are almost no exact lower bounds further analyzing and assessing existing upper bounds. This paper aims to solve these problems, with detailed comparisons provided in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work mainly provides precise approximation error upper and lower bounds for Hölder class with standard Transformers, delivering <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">three fundamental contributions</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A complete framework for approximation error upper bounds for Hölder class with standard Transformers.</span> The expressive power of standard architectures is characterized through the approximation theory without relying on model transformations, addressing two key challenges:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding of the capability of self-attention layers. Building on the significant single-layer Softmax-based contextual mapping in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>, we avoid transformations between Hardmax and Softmax operators, and recognize that the self-attention mechanism serves to memorize and differentiate tokens within input sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preservation of all residual connections. Residual connections are crucial in practical tasks but remain difficult to analyze theoretically due to the deep composition of blocks. We retain all residual connections by employing a novel network construction strategy. This ensures our theoretical results can be applied directly to the standard architectures used in practice and thereby reflect Transformers’ inductive bias.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) <span id="S1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complementary approximation lower bounds</span>. We prove an approximation lower bound for standard Transformers by deriving a VC-dimension upper bound determined by the total number of model operations and parameters, based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthony &amp; Bartlett, 2009</a>)</cite>. This derived lower bound opens a new direction for future research, advancing the approximation theory of Transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">iii</span>) <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Application on a general regression task.</span> We investigate the applicability of Transformers in finite-sample regression tasks of Hölder functions. The theoretical analysis demonstrates Transformer’s strong approximation capabilities and empirical effectiveness in real-world settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Universality and Approximation Upper Bounds.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Analogous to classical results that sufficiently large Feed-forward Neural Networks (FNNs) can accurately approximate broad function classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cybenko, 1989</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hornik et al., 1989</a>)</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2020)</a></cite> derived the first Universal Approximation Theorem (UAT) for standard Transformers on continuous sequence-to-sequence functions. Their work introduced a widely adopted framework which treats the self-attention mechanism as a contextual mapping and approximates an intermediate task of piece-wise constant functions using Transformers. Subsequent UAT work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite> streamlined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>)</cite>’s methodology by designing a single layer contextual mapping but modifying the architectures into Transformer variants without residual connections in feed-forward layers. UATs for constrained Transformers were established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kratsios et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kratsios, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To align the theoretical analysis of Transformers with that of FNNs, precise approximation upper bounds on model complexity are required. Upper bounds of a Transformer variant replacing softmax with Maximum operators were derived by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurevych et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takakura &amp; Suzuki (2023)</a></cite> developed a coordinate-wise rate for the specific <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function class (a Besov-like space) by treating the self-attention layer as a feature extractor for inputs, a formulation limiting the scope of their results. Based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>’s methodology, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite> obtained upper bounds for Lipschitz continuous functions, which which were subsequently extended to the broader Hölder class by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2026)</a></cite> achieved bounds for Hölder class through a distinctly different approach based on the Kolmogorov-Arnold Superposition Theorem. All the aforementioned works rely on Transformer variants that omit residual connections in the feed-forward layers. Notably, our construction of Transformers is inspired by these mentioned works excluding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takakura &amp; Suzuki, 2023</a>)</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and improves upon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>’s framework to derive an approximation upper bound for Hölder class with standard Transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximation Lower Bounds.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Approximation lower bounds determine whether corresponding upper bounds are optimal, thus guiding future research directions. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky &amp; Zhevnerchuk (2020)</a></cite> proved that their obtained approximation upper bounds of ReLU FNNs on Sobolev functions are nearly optimal (up to logarithmic factors) by deriving matching approximation lower bounds. Their analysis builds upon the VC-dimension upper bounds provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthony &amp; Bartlett, 2009</a>, Theorems 8.7-8.8)</cite>. Furthermore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2022)</a></cite> established the optimality (up to constants) of derived approximation upper bounds for ReLU FNNs on continuous functions, by leveraging the nearly-tight VC-dimension bounds of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. These results indicate the study of approximation bounds for ReLU FNNs has reached a mature stage. In recent years, lower bounds have also been established for other networks like Sigmoid networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barron, 1993</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel &amp; Xu, 2024</a>)</cite> and CNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, to the best of our knowledge, no lower bounds have been derived for Transformers until now. By analyzing the key VC-dimension upper bound provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthony &amp; Bartlett, 2009</a>, Theorem 8.14)</cite>, we may be the first to establish an approximation lower bound for deep Transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memorization Capacity of Transformers.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, theoretical memorization capacity of networks also contributes to appropriate model size selections, aligning with the principles of approximation bounds. Related studies have explored this for various architectures, including FNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baum, 1988</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vardi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, CNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen &amp; Hein, 2018</a>)</cite>, and Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahdavi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2025</a>)</cite>. Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2023)</a></cite> proved that Transformers consisting of ReLU activation feed-forward layers and a single-head attention layer, can memorize <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequences of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional tokens using <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(d+n+\sqrt{n}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(d+n+\sqrt{n}N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is used to hide logarithmic factors, while <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> ignores constant factors.</span></span></span> parameters. Meanwhile, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahdavi et al. (2024)</a></cite> isolated the role of multi-head attention, showing that a single <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-head attention layer with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Hd^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Hd^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> parameters can memorize <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Hn)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Hn)</annotation></semantics></math> examples. Refining these results, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato (2025)</a></cite> derived nearly optimal memorization rates, demonstrating that Transformers can memorize <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequences of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{nN})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{nN})</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. Results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2025</a>)</cite> build on the concept of contextual mapping introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which plays a pivotal role in understanding Transformer memorization capacity. While our work does not directly analyze this aspect, future research leveraging our architectural insights might lead to advancements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notations.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}_{+}\coloneq\left\{1,2,3,\ldots\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}_{+}\coloneq\left\{1,2,3,\ldots\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of positive integers with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}\coloneq\mathbb{N}_{+}\cup\{0\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}\coloneq\mathbb{N}_{+}\cup\{0\}.</annotation></semantics></math> Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the set of real numbers by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the non-negative subset by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\lesssim B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\lesssim B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\gtrsim A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≳</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\gtrsim A</annotation></semantics></math> to denote <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leq cB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leq cB</annotation></semantics></math> for some absolute constant <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\asymp B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≍</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\asymp B</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\lesssim B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\lesssim B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\gtrsim B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≳</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\gtrsim B</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}_{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}_{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector and the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times L</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with all one entries. For a vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert x\right\rVert_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert x\right\rVert_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p\in[1,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[1,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-norm and Frobenius norm respectively. For a scalar measurable function <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, its <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|f\right\|_{{2}}\coloneq(\int(f(x))^{2}\mathrm{d}x)^{1/2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|f\right\|_{{2}}\coloneq(\int(f(x))^{2}\mathrm{d}x)^{1/2}.</annotation></semantics></math> Similarly, for a sequence-to-sequence (seq-to-seq) function <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L},</annotation></semantics></math> we define <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|f\right\|_{2}\coloneq(\int\left\|f({X})\right\|_{F}^{2}\mathrm{d}{X})^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|f\right\|_{2}\coloneq(\int\left\|f({X})\right\|_{F}^{2}\mathrm{d}{X})^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 The Summary of Notations ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Notes ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> summarizes the notations for quick-reference and cross-checking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target Function Space.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the multi-index <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{n}=(n_{1},\ldots,n_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n}=(n_{1},\ldots,n_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-derivative of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{\bm{n}}f\coloneq(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{1}})^{n_{1}}\cdots(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{d}})^{n_{d}}f." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\bm{n}}f\coloneq(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{1}})^{n_{1}}\cdots(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{d}})^{n_{d}}f.</annotation></semantics></math> We use the convention that <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\bm{n}}f=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\bm{n}}f=f</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bm{n}\right\|_{1}=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bm{n}\right\|_{1}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hölder Class)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x},d_{y},K\in\mathbb{N}_{+},\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x},d_{y},K\in\mathbb{N}_{+},\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=r+\alpha_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=r+\alpha_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the Hölder class with smoothness index <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and norm constraint parameter <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{x},d_{y}}\left(\mathcal{X},K\right)\coloneq\Big\{{f}=\left(f_{1},\ldots,f_{d_{y}}\right)^{\top}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}\mid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{x},d_{y}}\left(\mathcal{X},K\right)\coloneq\Big\{{f}=\left(f_{1},\ldots,f_{d_{y}}\right)^{\top}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}\mid</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\underset{\left\lVert\bm{n}\right\rVert_{1}\leq r}{\max}\underset{{x}\in\mathcal{X}}{\sup}\left|\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({x})\right|\leq K," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒏</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\underset{\left\lVert\bm{n}\right\rVert_{1}\leq r}{\max}\underset{{x}\in\mathcal{X}}{\sup}\left|\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({x})\right|\leq K,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle~\underset{\left\lVert\bm{n}\right\rVert_{1}={r}}{\max}\underset{{x,y}\in\mathcal{X},{x}\neq{y}}{\sup}\frac{\left|\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({x})-\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({y})\right|}{\left\|{x}-{y}\right\|^{\alpha_{0}}_{2}}\leq K,~k\in[d_{y}]\Big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒏</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></mrow></munder><munder accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">≠</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\underset{\left\lVert\bm{n}\right\rVert_{1}={r}}{\max}\underset{{x,y}\in\mathcal{X},{x}\neq{y}}{\sup}\frac{\left|\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({x})-\partial^{\bm{n}}f_{k}({y})\right|}{\left\|{x}-{y}\right\|^{\alpha_{0}}_{2}}\leq K,~k\in[d_{y}]\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without loss of generality, we consider a bounded input domain <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> in this paper. Furthermore, we use <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)\coloneq\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)\coloneq\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x}=d_{y}=d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x}=d_{y}=d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07463v1/pipelineofTransformer.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/90;" width="452" height="90"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pipeline of the standard Transformer function class: bridging the vector form and sequence form.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformer Architecture.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) A Transformer block</span></span> consists of a dot-product Softmax self-attention layer, a ReLU feed-forward layer, and a residual connection per layer. For the input sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> length and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional tokens, a Transformer block mapping from <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\text{Attn}({X})={X}\!+\!\sum_{i=1}^{r_{1}}{W}^{i}_{O}{W}^{i}_{V}{X}\sigma_{S}\left[({W}^{i}_{K}{X})^{\top}{W}^{i}_{Q}{X}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\text{Attn}({X})={X}\!+\!\sum_{i=1}^{r_{1}}{W}^{i}_{O}{W}^{i}_{V}{X}\sigma_{S}\left[({W}^{i}_{K}{X})^{\top}{W}^{i}_{Q}{X}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{FF}\circ\text{Attn}({X})=\text{Attn}({X})+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>FF</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mtext>Attn</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{FF}\circ\text{Attn}({X})=\text{Attn}({X})+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad+\sum_{i=1}^{r_{2}}{W}_{2}^{i}\sigma_{R}\left[{W}_{1}^{i}\cdot\text{Attn}({X})+{b}_{1}^{i}\mathds{1}_{L}^{\top}\right]+{b}_{2}\mathds{1}_{d}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad+\sum_{i=1}^{r_{2}}{W}_{2}^{i}\sigma_{R}\left[{W}_{1}^{i}\cdot\text{Attn}({X})+{b}_{1}^{i}\mathds{1}_{L}^{\top}\right]+{b}_{2}\mathds{1}_{d}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}_{K}^{i},{W}_{Q}^{i},{W}_{V}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}_{K}^{i},{W}_{Q}^{i},{W}_{V}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are the key, query and value weight matrices, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}_{O}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}_{O}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the connection matrix, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}_{1}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{l\times d},{W}_{2}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times l},{b}_{1}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{l},{b}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}_{1}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{l\times d},{W}_{2}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times l},{b}_{1}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{l},{b}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterize the feed-forward layer. The Softmax operator <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> applies column-wisely with the ReLU activation <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> applying element-wisely. Indices <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the head number of attention layer and the neuron number of feed-forward layer, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) A Transformer network (encoder)</span></span> is a composition of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer blocks additionally with a positional encoding <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.E3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"><math id="S3.E3.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} \mathcal{T}^{D}&amp;\!\coloneq\left\{f_{\mathcal{T}}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\right\},~~\text{where}~~\\
~f_{\mathcal{T}}(X)&amp;={\rm FF}^{(D)}\!\!\circ\!{\rm Attn}^{(D)}\!\circ\!\cdots\!\circ\!{\rm FF}^{(1)}\!\circ\!{\rm Attn}^{(1)}(X\!+\!E).~~~~\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>FF</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>Attn</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.026em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.026em" rspace="0.026em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.026em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>FF</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>Attn</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} \mathcal{T}^{D}&amp;\!\coloneq\left\{f_{\mathcal{T}}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\right\},~~\text{where}~~\\
~f_{\mathcal{T}}(X)&amp;={\rm FF}^{(D)}\!\!\circ\!{\rm Attn}^{(D)}\!\circ\!\cdots\!\circ\!{\rm FF}^{(1)}\!\circ\!{\rm Attn}^{(1)}(X\!+\!E).~~~~\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Given that the function approximation task is defined on vector inputs, whereas Transformer networks operate on sequences, we introduce a reshape layer to rearrange the input vectors to the sequence format, additionally with the reverse flatten layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reshape and Flatten Layers)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a reshape layer that transforms the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional input into a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times L</annotation></semantics></math> sequence matrix with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides, the corresponding reverse flatten layer is defined by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{-1}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{-1}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the inverse of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">iii<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></span> <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Transformer function class</span> considered throughout the paper is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\!\!\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}:=\left\{g=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{out}}\!\circ\!R^{-1}\!\circ\!f_{\mathcal{T}}\!\circ\!R\circ\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{in}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\!\!\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}:=\left\{g=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{out}}\!\circ\!R^{-1}\!\circ\!f_{\mathcal{T}}\!\circ\!R\circ\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{in}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{in}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{in}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input embedding and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{out}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{out}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output embedding. The overall pipeline is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Target Function Space. ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem Setting.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Function approximation tasks of Hölder class <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)</annotation></semantics></math> with Transformer function class <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> aim to study both upper and lower bounds of the approximation error<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            The approximation error characterizes the fundamental limit of model’s expressivity, For further details, refer to Remark <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark D.2 (Roles of Each Error). ‣ D.1 Error Decomposition of Excess Risk ‣ Appendix D Details of Applications in the Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}\left(\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha},\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}\right)\coloneq\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha}}{\sup}\underset{g\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\inf}\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>app</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">D</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}\left(\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha},\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}\right)\coloneq\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{x},d_{y}}^{\alpha}}{\sup}\underset{g\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\inf}\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In a mainstream way, analysis of the approximation error equals providing upper and lower bounds of Transformer complexities when the error meets the minor accuracy criterion <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>app</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the input and output embeddings do not impact the core architecture of Transformer networks (See Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Target Function Space. ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we assume <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x}=d_{y}=d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x}=d_{y}=d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and omit the embeddings in this section for the sake of simplicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Upper Bound of Approximation Error)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1],d_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+},d_{0}&gt;2\alpha," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1],d_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+},d_{0}&gt;2\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any target function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)</annotation></semantics></math> and any error criterion <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Transformer function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of at most <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="S4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof Sketches</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We follow the basic pipeline of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>, Theorem 2)</cite> but introduce differences in constructions to ensure results for standard Transformers. The proof is divided roughly into three steps.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0em" mathvariant="bold">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1.}</annotation></semantics></math> Transfer to an intermediate approximation task. <span id="S4.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span><span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">) For the target Hölder class <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be reshaped into an equivalent sequence function class <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span><span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">) We approximate any <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}\in\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}\in\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> by a piece-wise constant function <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> with cell width <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> and separation width <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining the error <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\|{\bar{f}-f_{\delta}}\big\|_{2}&lt;\frac{\varepsilon}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|{\bar{f}-f_{\delta}}\big\|_{2}&lt;\frac{\varepsilon}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the regularity of Hölder class.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{2.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟐</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0em" mathvariant="bold">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{2.}</annotation></semantics></math> Approximate <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{H}(\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{H}(\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math> by designed Transformers.<span id="S4.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate it under <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\varepsilon}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\varepsilon}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error with the standard Transformer network <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}}=f_{\mathcal{T}3}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}2}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}1}\in\mathcal{T}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}}=f_{\mathcal{T}3}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}2}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}1}\in\mathcal{T}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> having three main parts,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span><span id="S4.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text">) Quantization module <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward layers and a positional encoding <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, maps most of continuous inputs <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\bar{\mathcal{X}}=[0,1]^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\bar{\mathcal{X}}=[0,1]^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> into discrete grid points, and constructs a positional grid set
<math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span><span id="S4.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text">) Contextual mapping <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of a single self-attention layer, and enhances the separation property of points in <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> by generating unique contextual ids <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for grid points <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}^{p}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">iii</span><span id="S4.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text">) Value mapping <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}3}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward layers and maps grid points to the target outputs, taking the contextual ids as anchor values.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{3.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟑</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0em" mathvariant="bold">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{3.}</annotation></semantics></math> Verification of the final error.<span id="S4.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> By triangle inequality, we have <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert\leq\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\delta}\right\rVert+\left\lVert f_{\delta}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert&lt;\varepsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert\leq\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\delta}\right\rVert+\left\lVert f_{\delta}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert&lt;\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math> Furthermore, with the reshape and flatten layers <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R,R^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R,R^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and equivalent norm transformations, the final result <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert f-g\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained.
∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> establishes that a standard Transformer, composed of at most <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks with a single active self-attention layer, serves as an effective approximator of Hölder functions. Increasing the Transformer blocks enhances the model’s expressive power. The primary challenge in approximating Hölder functions arises from the ratio between input dimension <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and smoothness index <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, we assume <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}&gt;2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}&gt;2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure a positive minor term <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is discussed later. Furthermore, the norm constraint <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for the Hölder class primarily serves as a stability condition for proof construction, independent of both the block number <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and error <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bounded Width)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, our construction employs a fixed-width architecture where inner dimensions <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{1}\cdot m,r_{2}\cdot l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{1}\cdot m,r_{2}\cdot l\}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, independent of both the smoothness <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and the target accuracy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the approximation bound is characterized exclusively by the block number <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Depth-width Trade-off)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL</annotation></semantics></math>, a Transformer architecture <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks and fixed width <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=4d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=4d</annotation></semantics></math>, which is capable of approximating any function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth index <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> under error <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, can be realized by a Transformer <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D^{\prime},W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D^{\prime},W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> comprising variable blocks <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, width <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and two additional linear embedding layers, satisfying the condition <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha}),</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}\geq 4d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}\geq 4d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">This corollary reveals a flexible depth-width trade-off. Rather than strictly adhering to the fixed-width regime in Theorem
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, one can achieve the same bound using a fixed-depth architecture with variable width, or more generally, an architecture where both depth and width vary simultaneously, as long as the product satisfies <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The following sections highlight the distinctive features of our Transformer architectures for approximation tasks. See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for full details and proofs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Approximation for Hölder Class by Piece-wise Constant Function Class</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any target function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the reshaped input set as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{X}}\coloneq\left\{X=R(x)\mid X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L},x\in\mathcal{X},x=R^{-1}(X)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{X}}\coloneq\left\{X=R(x)\mid X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L},x\in\mathcal{X},x=R^{-1}(X)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the reshape and flatten layers. We denote the reshaped target Hölder class by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}\coloneq\left\{\bar{f}=R\circ f\circ R^{-1}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}\coloneq\left\{\bar{f}=R\circ f\circ R^{-1}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Now we define the intermediate piece-wise constant class <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> approximating the reshaped Hölder class <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantization Grid and Cube)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,L\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,L\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the cell width <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and the separation width <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the number of grid points per dimension can be defined as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. And the grid point set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}\coloneq\left\{0,\delta+\delta^{*},2(\delta+\delta^{*}),\ldots,(M-1)(\delta+\delta^{*})\right\}^{d\times L}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}\coloneq\left\{0,\delta+\delta^{*},2(\delta+\delta^{*}),\ldots,(M-1)(\delta+\delta^{*})\right\}^{d\times L}.</annotation></semantics></math> For any grid point <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, its associated hypercube is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{G}\coloneq\prod_{j\in[d],k\in[L]}[G_{j,k},G_{j,k}+\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{G}\coloneq\prod_{j\in[d],k\in[L]}[G_{j,k},G_{j,k}+\delta]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By construction, the union of these hypercubes satisfies <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}\subset[0,1]^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}\subset[0,1]^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>. And any two cells maintain a separation margin of at least <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> along each coordinate. Formally, for any <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j,k}\neq G^{\prime}_{j,k}\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j,k}\neq G^{\prime}_{j,k}\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, their corresponding cells satisfy <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\min}([G_{j,k},G_{j,k}+\delta],[G^{\prime}_{j,k},G^{\prime}_{j,k}+\delta])\geq\delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\min}([G_{j,k},G_{j,k}+\delta],[G^{\prime}_{j,k},G^{\prime}_{j,k}+\delta])\geq\delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This separation margin is crucial for our constructive proof for ReLU-based Transformers, as it guarantees the necessary geometric margin for the subsequent theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Piece-wise Constant Function Class)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}\{\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}\{\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indicator function.
For each grid point <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, its hypercube <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its output matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{G}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{G}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, the piece-wise constant function class with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in[0,1]^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in[0,1]^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}(\delta)\coloneq\Big\{f_{\delta}=\sum\nolimits_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\!\!Y_{G}\cdot\mathds{1}\{X\in\mathcal{S}_{G}\}\Big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}(\delta)\coloneq\Big\{f_{\delta}=\sum\nolimits_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\!\!Y_{G}\cdot\mathds{1}\{X\in\mathcal{S}_{G}\}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Approximation Error between <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1],d_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1],d_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL&gt;2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL&gt;2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. For any given <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a target function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}\in\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}\in\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}\in\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}\!=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}\!=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{f}-f_{\delta}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{f}-f_{\delta}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inequality condition <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}&gt;2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}&gt;2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> can be easily satisfied since <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, with its purpose to ensure that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> remains strictly positive in the proof. In a sense, this highlights that our work can handle a harder approximation task with large input dimension <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and small function smoothness <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To approximate the piece-wise constant function class <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Transformer network <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}}\in\mathcal{T}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}}\in\mathcal{T}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises three primary parts: the quantization module, contextual mapping and value mapping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantization Module <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\delta^{*}\in(0,1),M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\delta^{*}\in(0,1),M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL</annotation></semantics></math>. By processing the inputs <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a quantization module <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}M</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward layers and a positional encoding <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\mathds{1}_{d}\cdot[1,2,\ldots,L]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\mathds{1}_{d}\cdot[1,2,\ldots,L]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, constructing a quantization positional grid <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}=\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}=\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}+E</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The quantization module <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> maps most of received continuous input sequences from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> to discrete grid points in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. For input sequences in the complement set <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times L}\setminus\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times L}\setminus\bigcup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}}\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, outputs of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> remain controllable, since <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded and the measure of the complement set is <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor)^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor)^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> which will be negligible with sufficiently small <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To construct the next contextual mapping <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the positional grid <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first introduce several necessary concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Token Vocabulary)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(1)},\ldots,X^{(N)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(1)},\ldots,X^{(N)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> input sequences consisting of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> tokens and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> embedding dimension. The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th vocabulary set is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{(i)}=\bigcup_{k\in[L]}{X}_{:,k}^{(i)}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>⋃</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{(i)}=\bigcup_{k\in[L]}{X}_{:,k}^{(i)}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the whole vocabulary set is defined as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\bigcup_{i\in[N]}\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\bigcup_{i\in[N]}\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Token-wise Separatedness)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\max}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\max},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> input sequences <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}^{(1)},\ldots,{X}^{(N)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}^{(1)},\ldots,{X}^{(N)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> are token-wise <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{\min},r_{\max},\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{\min},r_{\max},\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-separated if
the following conditions hold,</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math>, the boundedness <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{\min}\leq\big\|{X_{:,k}^{(i)}}\big\|_{2}\leq r_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>min</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\min}\leq\big\|{X_{:,k}^{(i)}}\big\|_{2}\leq r_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,l\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,l\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{:,k}^{(i)}\neq X_{:,l}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{:,k}^{(i)}\neq X_{:,l}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\|{X_{:,k}^{(i)}-X_{:,l}^{(j)}}\big\|_{2}\geq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|{X_{:,k}^{(i)}-X_{:,l}^{(j)}}\big\|_{2}\geq\beta</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem9" title="Definition 4.9 (Token-wise Separatedness). ‣ 4.2 Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> only considers separatedness among different tokens. As first clarified in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, sequence models like Transformers should distinguish between different input sequences sharing same tokens. To address this limitation, we adopt the essential single-layer contextual mapping in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kajitsuka &amp; Sato, 2024</a>)</cite>, enabling Transformers to capture whole context of each sequence and generate unique context ids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Contextual Mapping <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> grid points <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{d},\sqrt{d}(L+1),\sqrt{d}(\delta+\delta^{*}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{d},\sqrt{d}(L+1),\sqrt{d}(\delta+\delta^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math>-separated. A single self-attention layer equipped with one Softmax head, can be an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>-contextual mapping <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> for the input sequences <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[M^{d_{0}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[M^{d_{0}}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the boundedness </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\|{f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(i)})_{:,k}}\big\|_{2}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|{f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(i)})_{:,k}}\big\|_{2}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[M^{d_{0}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[M^{d_{0}}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,l\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,l\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that either </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\neq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\neq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G_{:,k}^{(i)}\neq G_{:,l}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{:,k}^{(i)}\neq G_{:,l}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the separatedness </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\lVert f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(~i)})_{:,k}-f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(j)})_{:,l}\right\rVert_{2}\geq\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.270em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(~i)})_{:,k}-f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G^{(j)})_{:,l}\right\rVert_{2}\geq\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\sqrt{d}(L+1)+\frac{\sqrt{d}(\delta+\delta^{*})}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\sqrt{d}(L+1)+\frac{\sqrt{d}(\delta+\delta^{*})}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\exp\left(-\frac{L^{6}M^{4d_{0}}}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\exp\left(-\frac{L^{6}M^{4d_{0}}}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The case <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\neq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{(i)}\neq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> in the second condition includes the scenario of different sequences sharing some of same tokens. Consequently, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math> maps all columns of grid points <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to the unique vectors named as contextual ids. With the help of the contextual mapping <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math>, next step of designing a one-to-one value mapping is feasible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Value Mapping <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}3}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output matrix of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\delta^{*}\in(0,1),M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor,d,L\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\delta^{*}\in(0,1),M=\lfloor\frac{1}{\delta+\delta^{*}}\rfloor,d,L\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=dL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=dL</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a value mapping <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}3}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}3}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2LM^{d_{0}}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2LM^{d_{0}}+1</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward layers such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}3}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)=Y_{G},\forall G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}3}\circ f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)=Y_{G},\forall G\in\mathcal{G}_{\delta,\delta^{*}}^{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Anchored on the unique contextual id <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)_{:,k},k\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}(G)_{:,k},k\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math>, a subunit composed of two feed-forward layers can map this column to the target <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{G:,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{G:,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This indicates that our constructions of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LM^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LM^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> subunits identify target positions of inputs and assign the corresponding output values. Additionally, the extra layer processes the accumulated calculation results by preceding subunits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Verification)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}\in\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}\in\mathcal{\bar{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,L}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{0}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{0}L</annotation></semantics></math>. By choosing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}=\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{\frac{2}{d_{0}}+\frac{1}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Transformer network <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of at most <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\bar{f}-f_{\mathcal{T}}\right\rVert_{2}&lt;\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This result is the reshaped version of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, see proof in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Verification for the Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Novelty of Constructions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The novelty of our construction is two-fold. A direct comparison with prior methods is provided in Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ B.3.1 Quantization Module 𝑓_{𝒯⁢1} ‣ B.3 Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers ‣ Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) For the quantization module <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}1}</annotation></semantics></math> and value mapping <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}3}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of feed-forward layers, our constructions are novel and unique since all residual connections and ReLU operators are kept, thereby reflecting the inductive bias of standard Transformers used in practice. For the constructions detail, refer to Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3.SSS1" title="B.3.1 Quantization Module 𝑓_{𝒯⁢1} ‣ B.3 Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers ‣ Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3.1</span></a> &amp; <a href="#A2.SS3.SSS3" title="B.3.3 Value Mapping 𝑓_{𝒯⁢3} ‣ B.3 Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers ‣ Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) For the contextual mapping <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}2}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of a single standard Softmax-based self-attention layer, the construction has shown that one activated attention layer is sufficient for strong theoretical approximation power. This result underscores the essential role of the attention mechanism’s separation enhancement property in distinguishing tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To complement our theoretical framework, further discussions on the connections between theoretical assumptions and practice are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Further Discussions: Assumptions, Implications, and Practical Architectures ‣ Appendix B Approximation Error Upper Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we derive the approximation error lower bound via a VC-dimension upper bound of the Transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(VC-dimension)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an indicator function class <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The VC-dimension of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the largest integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a sample set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S=\left\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\mid x_{i}\in\mathcal{X},i\in[n]\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\left\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\mid x_{i}\in\mathcal{X},i\in[n]\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> where the restriction <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}|_{S}=\{[m(x_{1}),\ldots,m(x_{n})]\mid m\in\mathcal{M},x_{i}\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|_{S}=\{[m(x_{1}),\ldots,m(x_{n})]\mid m\in\mathcal{M},x_{i}\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math> has cardinality <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathcal{M}|_{S}\right|=2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathcal{M}|_{S}\right|=2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrarily large, then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, the VC-dimension quantifies the hypothesis space’s maximum capacity to shatter sample points. It is worth analyzing the upper bound of the VC-dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthony &amp; Bartlett, 2009</a>, Theorem 8.14</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is a function from <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{\omega}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{\omega}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\left\{x\mapsto m(a,x)\mid a\in\mathbb{R}^{\omega},x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\left\{x\mapsto m(a,x)\mid a\in\mathbb{R}^{\omega},x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the class determined by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> parameters and input <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by an algorithm that takes as input the pair <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,x)</annotation></semantics></math> and returns <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(a,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(a,x)</annotation></semantics></math> after no more than <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> operations of the following types:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the exponential function </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\mapsto e^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\mapsto e^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on real numbers.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the arithmetic operations </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="/" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>/</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">/</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on real numbers.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the jumps conditioned on </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≠</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> comparisons of real numbers.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">output </span><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})\leq t^{2}\omega(\omega+19\log_{2}(9\omega))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})\leq t^{2}\omega(\omega+19\log_{2}(9\omega)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">VC-dimension upper bound (<a href="#S5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Theorem 5.2 ( , , Theorem 8.14). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) is determined by the total number of operations and parameters constituting the model space <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Particularly, it takes into account the exponential function operations, which captures the operational characteristics of Transformers’ Softmax operators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By convention of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, for a real-valued function class <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extend the VC-dimension by defining <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})\coloneq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathrm{sgn}(\mathcal{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{M})\coloneq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathrm{sgn}(\mathcal{M}))</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{sgn}(\mathcal{M})\coloneq\{\mathrm{sgn}(m)\mid m\in\mathcal{M}\},~\text{and}~\mathrm{sgn}(x)=\mathds{1}[x&gt;0]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>sgn</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mi>sgn</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sgn}(\mathcal{M})\coloneq\{\mathrm{sgn}(m)\mid m\in\mathcal{M}\},~\text{and}~\mathrm{sgn}(x)=\mathds{1}[x&gt;0].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The application of <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sgn}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sgn}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> only introduces a single thresholding operation (an extra jump on “<math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>” in item <span id="S5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">3</span> of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 ( , , Theorem 8.14). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>), while leaves the parameter number <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged. Therefore, we can directly apply the Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 ( , , Theorem 8.14). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> to the standard Transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower Bound of Approximation Error)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a Transformer architecture <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, which is capable of approximating any function <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0}}^{\alpha}(\mathcal{X},K)</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth index <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> under error <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, must possess <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> operations, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> parameters and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> blocks satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\!D^{4}\gtrsim t^{2}\omega(\omega+19\log_{2}(9\omega))\geq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\!D^{4}\gtrsim t^{2}\omega(\omega+19\log_{2}(9\omega))\geq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, the block number <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketches.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is divided into two steps. For details, see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Approximation Error Lower Bound ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0em" mathvariant="bold">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1.}</annotation></semantics></math> VC-dimension of Transformers.<span id="S5.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Due to the detail-oriented structure of constructed Transformers for the approximation upper bound, we can directly derive that <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> share the same order of magnitude as the block number <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\omega\sim D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\omega\sim D</annotation></semantics></math>. With Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 ( , , Theorem 8.14). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we have <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\lesssim D^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\lesssim D^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{2.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟐</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0em" mathvariant="bold">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{2.}</annotation></semantics></math> Error lower bounds.<span id="S5.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span><span id="S5.p7.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span><span id="S5.p7.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) The VC-dimension formally quantifies the maximum memorization capacity of the hypothesis space. </span><span id="S5.p7.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii</span><span id="S5.p7.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) Once the approximation of a special Hölder classification function on <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> points is achieved, we show that Transformers can at least shatter those points, which means <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D})\gtrsim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3 (Lower Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> establishes the theoretic limit of standard Transformers in approximation theory, which implies such architectures must possess at least <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tightness Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> &amp; <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3 (Lower Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, there is a gap between the lower and upper bounds, i.e., <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}}\lesssim D\lesssim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>≲</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{4\alpha}}\lesssim D\lesssim\varepsilon^{-\frac{d_{0}}{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which suggests scope for improvement:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) The current token-to-token value mapping of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 4.11 (Value Mapping 𝑓_{𝒯⁢3}). ‣ 4.2 Approximation for Piece-wise Constant Function Class by Standard Transformers ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a> fails to exploit the higher smoothness of the target function class when <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus increases the Transformer blocks unnecessarily. This stems from the inherent challenge of constructing the piece-wise polynomials via parallel sequence feed-forward layers in standard Transformers, which is a prevalent problem in the field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) The VC-dimension upper bound (<a href="#S5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Theorem 5.2 ( , , Theorem 8.14). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) should be tighter. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2022)</a></cite> has proven the optimal lower bound only for deep ReLU FNNs via a nearly-tight VC-dimension result. However, even today, upper and lower bounds of networks incorporating other operators, especially the Sigmoid-like<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Operators involve exponential operations (e.g., <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-x}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></span> ones, remain inconsistent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While a gap remains between the upper and lower bounds, to the best of our knowledge, this work establishes the first quantitative characterization of the approximation capability of standard Transformers, serving as a landmark that can inspire a host of subsequent studies. We leave tightening these bounds as a promising direction for future research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(VC-dimension Lower Bound)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the class of fixed-width, depth <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward networks, denoted by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a strict subset of the Transformer function class <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. This structural inclusion inherently yields <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}^{D}_{R})\geq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{F}^{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}^{D}_{R})\geq\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{F}^{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al. (2019)</a></cite> established that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{F}^{D})=\Omega(D^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{F}^{D})=\Omega(D^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}^{D}_{R})=\Omega(D^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VCdim}(\mathcal{T}^{D}_{R})=\Omega(D^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, fully closing the error gap to match the desired approximation lower bound of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/(2\alpha)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\varepsilon^{-d_{0}/(2\alpha)})</annotation></semantics></math>, a tighter VC-dimension upper bound approaching this <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(D^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(D^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> rate is required.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Applications in a General Regression Task</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we apply obtained results to a general regression task, demonstrating Transformer’s approximation capabilities and effectiveness in real-world settings .</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regression Tasks.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose the random variable pair <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y)\sim\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y)\sim\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint distribution on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}.</annotation></semantics></math> Based on the finite i.i.d dataset <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{D}=\{z^{(1)},\ldots,z^{(N)}\mid z^{(i)}=(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}=\{z^{(1)},\ldots,z^{(N)}\mid z^{(i)}=(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the regression task is to estimate the target Hölder function <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=\!\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}\!(\mathcal{X},K)}{\arg\min}\mathcal{L}(f)=\!\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}\!(\mathcal{X},K)}{\arg\min}\mathbb{E}_{z}\left[\left(f(x)-y\right)^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">K</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">K</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=\!\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}\!(\mathcal{X},K)}{\arg\min}\mathcal{L}(f)=\!\underset{f\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}\!(\mathcal{X},K)}{\arg\min}\mathbb{E}_{z}\left[\left(f(x)-y\right)^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the population risk under the distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sample pairs, we define the empirical risk minimizer <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> over the Transformer function class <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (with embedding dimensions <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x}=d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x}=d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) as</p>
<table id="S6.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}(x)=\underset{f\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\arg\min}~\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(f(x^{(i)})-y^{(i)}\right)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">D</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}(x)=\underset{f\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\arg\min}~\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(f(x^{(i)})-y^{(i)}\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By introducing an optimization solver <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the actual estimator <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s(\tau)}\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s(\tau)}\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S6.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})\leq\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(\hat{f})+\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})\leq\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(\hat{f})+\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimization error from the solver <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the expectation of excess risk which evaluates the average performance of the estimator <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S6.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]=\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-\mathcal{L}(f^{*})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]=\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-\mathcal{L}(f^{*})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Further we can decompose (<a href="#S6.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) into three parts<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            See the error decomposition details in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Error Decomposition of Excess Risk ‣ Appendix D Details of Applications in the Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</span></span></span> like</p>
<table id="S6.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]\leq\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}+2\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}^{2}+2\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sta</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>app</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]\leq\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}+2\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}^{2}+2\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}=\underset{p\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}}{\sup}\underset{f\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\inf}\left\lVert f-p\right\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>app</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">sup</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">D</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}=\underset{p\in\mathcal{H}_{d_{0},1}^{\alpha}}{\sup}\underset{f\in\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}}{\inf}\left\lVert f-p\right\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the approximation error and the statistical error is defined as .</p>
<table id="S6.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}\coloneq\mathbb{E_{D}}[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-2\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})+\mathcal{L}(f^{*})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sta</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}\coloneq\mathbb{E_{D}}[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-2\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})+\mathcal{L}(f^{*})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">The upper bound of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>app</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{app}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Now we derive the upper bound of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sta</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Upper Bound of Statistical Error)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and sample size <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the Transformer function class <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{0},1}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{0},1}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)</annotation></semantics></math>, the statistical error <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}=\mathbb{E_{D}}[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-2\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})+\mathcal{L}(f^{*})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sta</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}=\mathbb{E_{D}}[\mathcal{L}(f_{s(\tau)})-2\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(f_{s(\tau)})+\mathcal{L}(f^{*})]</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S6.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\!\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}\lesssim\frac{(16K+8)(D^{4}\log(eKND^{-4}/\mu)+1)}{N}+3\mu," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sta</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\!\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sta}}\lesssim\frac{(16K+8)(D^{4}\log(eKND^{-4}/\mu)+1)}{N}+3\mu,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the adjustable radius parameter.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Upper Bound of Statistical Error). ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> characterizes the finite-sample error of estimator <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> given a fixed Transformer. Intuitively, this error approaches <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with a decaying <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, proving this statistical error upper bound amounts to bounding the uniform covering number of Transformers given a finite sample size, which leverages our derived novel VC-dimension upper bound. (For details, refer to Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Upper Bound of the Statistical Error ‣ Appendix D Details of Applications in the Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> &amp; Remark <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem7" title="Remark D.7 (Novelty of Proof). ‣ Bounding the Covering Number. ‣ D.2 Upper Bound of the Statistical Error ‣ Appendix D Details of Applications in the Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence Rate of Excess Risk)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the regression function <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{0},1}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{\alpha}_{d_{0},1}([0,1]^{d_{0}},K)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the estimator over the i.i.d samples <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk bound holds,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]-2\tau\lesssim N^{-\frac{\alpha}{2d_{0}+\alpha}}\log(N)+N^{-\frac{\alpha}{2d_{0}+\alpha}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E_{D}}\left[\mathcal{E}(f_{s(\tau)})\right]-2\tau\lesssim N^{-\frac{\alpha}{2d_{0}+\alpha}}\log(N)+N^{-\frac{\alpha}{2d_{0}+\alpha}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimization error.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketches.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">With Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Upper Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 4 Approximation Error Upper Bound for Hölder Class with Transformers ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> &amp; <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Upper Bound of Statistical Error). ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> and equation (<a href="#S6.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), set <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=D^{-\frac{2\alpha}{d_{0}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=D^{-\frac{2\alpha}{d_{0}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\asymp N^{\frac{d_{0}}{4d_{0}+2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\asymp N^{\frac{d_{0}}{4d_{0}+2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the result is proved.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For this regression task, (<a href="#S6.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Theorem 6.2 (Convergence Rate of Excess Risk). ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) implies a trade-off between the approximation error (model complexity) and the statistical error (generalization ability) under finite <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples. With a fixed sample size <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess risk initially decreases and subsequently increases as the model complexity <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> grows. The optimal estimation case is achieved when the relation <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\asymp N^{\frac{d_{0}}{4d_{0}+2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\asymp N^{\frac{d_{0}}{4d_{0}+2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> holds. This insight provides crucial guidance for practical engineering design of Transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimization Error)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bounding the optimization error <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S6.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Theorem 6.2 (Convergence Rate of Excess Risk). ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is also necessary for a complete analysis of the excess risk, yet it remains highly challenging in theory at present. Importantly, our analysis holds independently of the optimization process, and we leave the analysis of optimization error <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for further work.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimality)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the minimax optimal convergence rate for deep ReLU networks in our regression setting is <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{-\frac{2\alpha}{d_{0}+2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{-\frac{2\alpha}{d_{0}+2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt-Hieber, 2020</a>)</cite>. Our derived rate in (<a href="#S6.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Theorem 6.2 (Convergence Rate of Excess Risk). ‣ Regression Tasks. ‣ 6 Applications in a General Regression Task ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is sub-optimal, primarily attributed to the employed loose VC-dimension upper bound (<a href="#S5.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 5.3 (Lower Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Specifically, the nearly-tight VC-dimension upper bound for fixed-width deep ReLU networks scales as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(D^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(D^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al., 2019</a>)</cite> while our bound scales as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D^{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D^{4})</annotation></semantics></math>. Narrowing this gap via the aspects discussed in “Tightness Analysis” part after Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3 (Lower Bound of Approximation Error). ‣ 5 Approximation Lower Bound with VC-dimension ‣ Approximation Error Upper and Lower Bounds for Hölder Class with Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> will promisingly yield a tighter convergence rate.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper establishes error upper and lower bounds for standard Transformers (with Softmax operators, ReLU activations and residual connections) in approximating the bounded Hölder class, thereby quantitatively revealing the fundamental expressive power of Transformers. For the upper bound <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-{d_{0}}/{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-{d_{0}}/{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> on the block number, we explicitly construct three key modules of standard Transformers: the quantization module, contextual mapping and value mapping; For the lower bound, a new yet non-optimal conclusion reveals that at least <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega(\varepsilon^{-{d_{0}}/({4\alpha})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\varepsilon^{-{d_{0}}/({4\alpha})})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks are required to achieve <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy. The error gap of two bounds highlights substantial room for approach improvement and can inspire the subsequent studies. We then derive excess risk rates of Transformers in regression tasks involving the Hölder class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis reveals several key theoretical challenges for further work: <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span>) Simplifying constructions of the value mapping and utilizing higher smoothness of target functions for tighter upper bounds. <span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) Tighter VC-dimension upper bounds to obtain (nearly) optimal approximation lower bounds. <span id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iii</span>) Role of compositions of multiple self-attention layers in the approximation tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents theoretical results regarding the approximation capabilities of standard Transformer architectures, contributing to the interpretability theory of Transformers. As a foundational work in statistical learning theory, its immediate societal consequences are primarily indirect. Understanding the theoretical model complexity requirements may help reduce the computational and environmental costs of training. And we acknowledge that our bounds provide theoretical limits and may differ from the empirical scale of current highly complex large language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
